{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b5bbb1f1784c84be", "type": "tool_result", "content": "/usr/bin/pdftotext\n1935 /tmp/p-muse.txt\nP-MUSE: Prompt-MIDI-Optional Model for Unified Instrumental Music\nSynthesis and Editing\nChong Jing1 , Junan Zhang1 , Jing Yang2 , Yulun Wu2 , Fan Fan2 , Zhizheng Wu1∗\n1\n\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shen Zhen\n2\nCentral Media Technology Institute, Huawei\nchongjing@link.cuhk.edu.cn, junanzhang@link.cuhk.edu.cn, yangjing201@huawei.com,\nwuyulun9@huawei.com, fanfan1@huawei.com, wuzhizheng@cuhk.edu.cn\n\narXiv:2608.01920v1 [cs.SD] 3 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nMIDI-to-Music system renders the melody and rhythm of a\ntarget MIDI sequence into musical segment while cloning instrument timbre from a prompt recording. Existing systems\ntypically adopt one of two distinct paradigms: conditional\ngeneration with prompt audio alone, which remains applicable when aligned prompt MIDI is unavailable, and In-Context\nLearning with paired prompt audio and MIDI, which exploits\ncross-modal alignment for stronger control on MIDI following\nand timbre similarity. We introduce P-MUSE, an instrumental MIDI-to-Music framework that unifies both paradigms via\na multi-stage Curriculum-Learning supporting prompt-MIDIoptional inputs. P-MUSE further unifies music generation and\nlocal editing through a shared fill-in-the-middle formulation.\nGrounded in theoretical analysis and empirical study, we propose a phase-aware classifier-free guidance scheduling principle for Transcription-to-Audio systems, alongside a Tail-Drop\nstrategy. Finally, to advance research in this field, we establish\nthe first comprehensive benchmark, covering various prompt\nmodes, generation/editing tasks, and four representative instruments: piano, guitar, bass, and drums. Demos are available\nat https://p-muse.github.io/.\n\nIntroduction\nTranscription-to-Audio systems generate audio segments\naligned with its transcriptions while preserving reference\nacoustic characteristics. As prominent instances of the\nTranscription-to-Audio system, MIDI-to-Music (MTM) renders target MIDI into musical segments in the timbre of\nprompt audio while Text-to-Speech (TTS) renders text into\nspeech in the timbre of the target speaker.\nExisting Transcription-to-Audio systems follow two complementary paradigms, depending on whether reference transcriptions are available: style prompting (Demerlé et al. 2024;\nKim et al. 2025; Casanova et al. 2023; Wang et al. 2023) conditions generation on target transcription (MIDI or Text) and\nprompt audio (Speech or Music) alone, offering robust and\nflexible inference when prompt transcription is unavailable;\npaired prompting (Tang et al. 2025; Cui et al. 2026; Jing\net al. 2026; Le et al. 2023; Eskimez et al. 2024; Chen et al.\n∗\n\nCorresponding author.\nCopyright © 2027, Association for the Advancement of Artificial\nIntelligence (www.aaai.org). All rights reserved.\n\nMulti-Stage Curriculum-Learning for Prompt-MIDI-Optional Adaption\n\nMixed Prompt\n\nStage 1:\nPretraining\nPaired Prompt\n\nStage 2:\nSFT for Style\nPrompt\n\nStage 3:\nSFT for Mixed\nPrompt\n\nStyle Prompt\nPrompt Audio\n\nPrompt MIDI\n\nWithout Prompt MIDI\n\nFigure 1: Multi-stage curriculum. Stage 1 learns pairedprompt In-Context Learning; Stage 2 adapts the model to\nstyle prompts through prompt-MIDI dropout; and Stage 3\ntrains for supporting mixed prompts while retaining compatibility with all three prompt modes.\n\n2025; Deng et al. 2025; Du et al. 2025, 2024) instead exploits\naligned transcription–audio context to provide stronger transcription following and more precise timbre similarity.\nUnifying these isolated paradigms into a single model is\nhighly desirable: it grants the system universal applicability\nacross heterogeneous inference scenarios and enables it to\nfully exploit all available reference information. Although\nrecent TTS architectures have bridged analogous paradigms\nby co-training speaker encoders alongside generative backbones (Hu et al. 2026; Zhang et al. 2025), directly transferring this strategy to MTM encounters severe performance\nbottlenecks. Direct joint optimization of the encoder suffers\nbecause existing open-source instrumental music datasets\nlack timbre diversity. This challenge is further exacerbated\nin MTM, where musical timbres exhibit far more complex\ndynamic envelopes and richer harmonic overtones than human speech. This limitation raises a fundamental question:\nHow can MTM seamlessly bridge the two Transcriptionto-Audio paradigms without being bottlenecked by the\nlimited timbre diversity of current music datasets?\nTo address this challenge, we introduce P-MUSE, a\nPrompt-MIDI-Optional Model for Unified Synthesis and\nEditing in instrumental music. As illustrated in Figure 1,\nP-MUSE adopts a progressive multi-stage CurriculumLearning strategy (Bengio et al. 2009; Graves et al. 2017). It\ninherits the strong MIDI following and timbre preservation\n\n\fpriors established during paired In-Context Learning (ICL)\npre-training, then progressively transfers these capabilities to\nthe unpaired style-prompt setting and ultimately the mixedprompt regime. This progressive capability transfer improves\ninput robustness while maximizing the use of available reference information. Furthermore, P-MUSE adopts a fill-inthe-middle (FIM) formulation (Bavarian et al. 2022) to unify", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "59a9c613-df33-4854-9aa8-2d380bdab217", "uuid": "7c11eb7b-3498-4670-b45d-c694da0e31fb", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T06:33:52.605Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "/usr/bin/pdftotext\n1935 /tmp/p-muse.txt\nP-MUSE: Prompt-MIDI-Optional Model for Unified Instrumental Music\nSynthesis and Editing\nChong Jing1 , Junan Zhang1 , Jing Yang2 , Yulun Wu2 , Fan Fan2 , Zhizheng Wu1∗\n1\n\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shen Zhen\n2\nCentral Media Technology Institute, Huawei\nchongjing@link.