on estimator (Nadaraya 1964). Let the data distribution be approximated by the empirical distribution over D = {xi1 }N\ni=1 :\n\nN\nX\ni=1\n\n(11)\n\nwhere x0 is independent of x1 . Solving the path equation for\nx0 gives\nxt − α t x1\nx0 =\n(12)\nσt\nSince the CFM conditional velocity is ut (xt | x1 ) = α̇t x1 +\nσ̇t x0 , substituting the expression above yields\nut (xt | x1 )\n\nE[x1 | xt ] =\n\n:=\n=\n\nE[ut (xt | x1 ) | xt , c]\nAt E[x1 | xt , c] + Bt xt\n\n(25)\n\nLet πi (c) = P (I = i | C = c) denote the conditional\nempirical prior over the finite dataset {(xi1 , ci )}N\ni=1 . Bayes’\nrule gives\n\u0010\n\u0011\n∥x −α xi ∥2\nπi (c) exp − t 2σt2 1 2\nt\n\u0010\n\u0011\nγi (xt , t, c) = P\n(26)\n∥xt −αt xj1 ∥22\nN\nπ\n(c)\nexp\n−\n2\nj\nj=1\n2σ\nt\n\nso that\nE[x1 | xt , c] =\n\nN\nX\n\nγi (xt , t, c)xi1\n\n(27)\n\ni=1\n\nCombining this posterior mean with Eq. 25 gives the general\nconditional velocity field\nu⋆cond (xt , t, c) = At\n\nN\nX\n\nγi (xt , t, c)xi1 + Bt xt\n\n(28)\n\ni=1\n\nUnder the rectified schedule, it becomes\nPN\nγi (xt , t, c)xi1 − xt\n(29)\nu⋆cond (xt , t, c) = i=1\n1−t\nFor a class-level condition, let Ic = {i : ci = c} be the\nselected subset. The conditional empirical prior is πi (c) =\n1/|Ic | for i ∈ Ic and πi (c) = 0 otherwise. The common\nfactor 1/|Ic | cancels in Eq. 26, yielding\n\u0010\n\u0011\n∥xt −txi1 ∥22\nexp − 2(1−t)\n2\n\u0011\n\u0010\n(30)\nγic (xt , t) = P\n∥xt −txj1 ∥22\nj∈Ic exp − 2(1−t)2\nfor i ∈ Ic . Substituting these weights into Eq. 29 gives\nP\nγic (xt , t)xi1 − xt\n⋆\nucond (xt , t, c) = i∈Ic\n(31)\n1−t\n\n\fInstrument group\n\nHours Timbres\n\n• The Expanded Groove MIDI Dataset (e-gmd) (Callender, Hawthorne, and Engel 2020) is a large-scale drum\ndataset with human drummer performances and velocity\nannotations, containing 444.5 hours.\n\nSegments\n\nBass\nDrum\nGuitar\nPiano\n\n1,969.17\n1,537.27\n1,073.67\n5,861.66\n\n40\n474,061\n34\n867,562\n25\n644,000\n117 1,449,904\n\nTotal\n\n10,441.77\n\n216 3,435,527\n\nTable 10: Final segmented training set after silence filtering\nand artifact removal.\nDataset\nNSynth\nSlakh\nSynthTab\ne-gmd\n\nTrain Timbres Total Timbres Held-out Timbres\n148\n10\n22\n34\n\n166\n13\n24\n43\n\n18\n3\n2\n9\n\nTable 11: Held-out timbre split used for benchmark construction.\n\nFor an instance-level condition, the condition uniquely\nk(c)\nidentifies one sample x1 . In this case, πi (c) = 1[i =\nk(c)], and Eq. 26 gives γi (xt , t, c) = 1[i = k(c)] for all t.\nTherefore, Eq. 29 reduces to\nk(c)\n\nu⋆cond (xt , t, c) =\n\nx1\n\n− xt\n1−t\n\n(32)\nk(c)\n\nSince the rectified path satisfies xt = (1 − t)x0 + tx1\nunder this condition, the same field can also be written as\nk(c)\n\nu⋆cond (xt , t, c) = x1\n\n− x0\n\n(33)\n\nAppendix B: Additional Dataset Details\nThis section provides additional details on the training\ndataset and the benchmark construction. P-MUSE uses five\nopen-source datasets with aligned MIDI and audio:\n• Slakh (Manilow et al. 2019) renders MIDI tracks with\nvirtual instruments, preserves separated stems, and mixes\nthem into complete multitrack songs. It contains 2,100\nfull songs, approximately 145 hours of mixed audio, 34\nbroad instrument categories, and 187 timbre patches.\n• GuitarSet (Xi et al. 2018) contains 3 hours of acousticguitar recordings performed by six guitarists. It uses hexaphonic pickups to record the six strings as separate channels, which removes cross-string mixture interference and\nsupports precise string-level pitch and timing annotation.\n• MAESTRO (Hawthorne et al. 2019) is collected from\nYamaha Disklavier piano performances, where the instrument records high-precision MIDI while the live performance is captured as audio. It provides more than 200\nhours of closely aligned piano audio and MIDI.\n• SynthTab (Zang et al. 2024) is a large synthetic guitaroriented corpus generated by rendering MIDI or tablature\nwith virtual instruments and synthesizers, covering 24\ntimbres and 7,684 hours of audio.\n\nWe additionally render MIDI performances with\nNSynth (Engel et al. 2017) as a sample-based synthesizer.\nThe original NSynth dataset contains acoustic, electronic,\nand synthetic timbre categories. We use only the acoustic\nand electronic categories, and separate single-track MIDI\nfiles from the Lakh MIDI Dataset (Raffel 2016) according to\nGeneral MIDI rules. Because NSynth provides only discrete\nvelocity levels, each MIDI velocity is quantized to its nearest available value in {25, 50, 75, 100, 127}. The matched\nNSynth recording with the same pitch and quantized velocity is then placed at the MIDI note onset, modulated by\nthe preset envelope and mixed into the output waveform. To\nsupport notes longer than the original sample, the sustain\nportion is extended by looping a fixed segment with crossfading, followed by attack and release envelopes to reduce\nartifacts. Rendered clips with missing pitch or velocity samples are discarded. In the NSynth rendering pipeline, we\nrender bass and piano tracks (with keyboards included under\npiano), alongside a few guitar tracks. Crucially, the rendered\nguitar audio is excluded from training and reserved exclusively for out-of-distribution (OOD) benchmark testing, as\nnote-based rendering lacks expressive performance dynamics and is thus ill-suited for training. For training, this rendering pipeline produces 36 bass timbres, including 1 acoustic\nand 35 electronic timbres, and 112 piano timbres, including\n18 acoustic and 94 electronic timbres.\nFull recordings are segmented into 3–30 s clips, with their\nmetadata recorded in a JSONL file. We apply voice-activity\ndetection to avoid silent segments. Since Slakh and SynthTab\nare synthetic corpora and have been reported by the research\nand open-source communities to contain occasional soundleakage artifacts, we remove the affected segments during\npre-processing. The final training dataset as shown in Table 10 contains 10,441.77 hours, 216 timbres, and 3,435,527\naudio/MIDI segments in total, averaging approximately 50\nhours per timbre after segmentation.\n\nAppendix c: Additional Benchmark Details\nThe benchmark is constructed from timbres held out from\ntraining (Table 11), as well as some guitar segments rendered\nusing NSynth. We construct three benchmarks for the paired,\nstyle, and mixed prompt settings. Each benchmark independently samples 3–30 s target audio/MIDI clips from the heldout timbres, with 100 clips per instrument group. Specifically, the paired and style prompt benchmarks share an identical composition, differing only in whether prompt MIDI\nis loaded at inference time. The mixed prompt benchmark\nadopts the same segments as the former two benchmarks\nbut with different slicing intervals, supplying both prompt\ntypes simultaneously. Each target clip defines one generation\ncase and five local-editing cases: note addition, note deletion,\npitch shift, velocity scaling, and timing perturbation. Specifically, generation follows a prefix-mid mode, whereas\nlocal editing adopts the standard prefix-mid-suffix\n\n\fHyperparameter\nTraining Dataset\nFIM Modes\nMIDI Drop Probability\nMIDI Drop Scope\nTraining Steps\nPeak Learning Rate\nWarmup Steps\n\nStage 1\n\nStage 2\n\nStage 3\n\nFull dataset (10,442 h)\nAll three modes†\n0.3\nAll\n400,000\n1 × 10−4\n32,000\n\nHigh-quality subset (400 h)\nprefix-mid\n0.5\nAll / Prompt\n9,000\n2 × 10−5\n4,000\n\nHigh-quality subset (400 h)\nAll three modes†\n0.3\nAll\n9,000\n2 × 10−5\n4,000\n\nTable 12: Detailed stage-wise curriculum training hyperparameters for P-MUSE. All three modes† include\nprefix-mid-suffix, prefix-mid, and mid-suffix.\nCurriculum Prompt FAD ↓ Sim. (%) ↑ F1 (%) ↑\nS1→S2→S3 Paired\nS1\nPaired\nS1→S2\nPaired\nS1→S3\nPaired\nDirect\nPaired\nS1→S2→S3 Style\nS1→S2\nStyle\nS1→S3\nStyle\nDirect\nStyle\nS1→S2→S3 Mixed\nS1→S3\nMixed\nDirect\nMixed\n\n0.835\n0.904\n0.839\n0.895\n0.763\n0.808\n0.852\n0.834\n0.871\n0.831\n0.893\n0.827\n\n93.1±.2 71.8±.8\n92.4±.2 70.9±.8\n92