e than 200\nhours of closely aligned piano audio and MIDI.\n• SynthTab (Zang et al. 2024) is a large synthetic guitaroriented corpus generated by rendering MIDI or tablature\nwith virtual instruments and synthesizers, covering 24\ntimbres and 7,684 hours of audio.\n\nWe additionally render MIDI performances with\nNSynth (Engel et al. 2017) as a sample-based synthesizer.\nThe original NSynth dataset contains acoustic, electronic,\nand synthetic timbre categories. We use only the acoustic\nand electronic categories, and separate single-track MIDI\nfiles from the Lakh MIDI Dataset (Raffel 2016) according to\nGeneral MIDI rules. Because NSynth provides only discrete\nvelocity levels, each MIDI velocity is quantized to its nearest available value in {25, 50, 75, 100, 127}. The matched\nNSynth recording with the same pitch and quantized velocity is then placed at the MIDI note onset, modulated by\nthe preset envelope and mixed into the output waveform. To\nsupport notes longer than the original sample, the sustain\nportion is extended by looping a fixed segment with crossfading, followed by attack and release envelopes to reduce\nartifacts. Rendered clips with missing pitch or velocity samples are discarded. In the NSynth rendering pipeline, we\nrender bass and piano tracks (with keyboards included under\npiano), alongside a few guitar tracks. Crucially, the rendered\nguitar audio is excluded from training and reserved exclusively for out-of-distribution (OOD) benchmark testing, as\nnote-based rendering lacks expressive performance dynamics and is thus ill-suited for training. For training, this rendering pipeline produces 36 bass timbres, including 1 acoustic\nand 35 electronic timbres, and 112 piano timbres, including\n18 acoustic and 94 electronic timbres.\nFull recordings are segmented into 3–30 s clips, with their\nmetadata recorded in a JSONL file. We apply voice-activity\ndetection to avoid silent segments. Since Slakh and SynthTab\nare synthetic corpora and have been reported by the research\nand open-source communities to contain occasional soundleakage artifacts, we remove the affected segments during\npre-processing. The final training dataset as shown in Table 10 contains 10,441.77 hours, 216 timbres, and 3,435,527\naudio/MIDI segments in total, averaging approximately 50\nhours per timbre after segmentation.\n\nAppendix c: Additional Benchmark Details\nThe benchmark is constructed from timbres held out from\ntraining (Table 11), as well as some guitar segments rendered\nusing NSynth. We construct three benchmarks for the paired,\nstyle, and mixed prompt settings. Each benchmark independently samples 3–30 s target audio/MIDI clips from the heldout timbres, with 100 clips per instrument group. Specifically, the paired and style prompt benchmarks share an identical composition, differing only in whether prompt MIDI\nis loaded at inference time. The mixed prompt benchmark\nadopts the same segments as the former two benchmarks\nbut with different slicing intervals, supplying both prompt\ntypes simultaneously. Each target clip defines one generation\ncase and five local-editing cases: note addition, note deletion,\npitch shift, velocity scaling, and timing perturbation. Specifically, generation follows a prefix-mid mode, whereas\nlocal editing adopts the standard prefix-mid-suffix\n\n\fHyperparameter\nTraining Dataset\nFIM Modes\nMIDI Drop Probability\nMIDI Drop Scope\nTraining Steps\nPeak Learning Rate\nWarmup Steps\n\nStage 1\n\nStage 2\n\nStage 3\n\nFull dataset (10,442 h)\nAll three modes†\n0.3\nAll\n400,000\n1 × 10−4\n32,000\n\nHigh-quality subset (400 h)\nprefix-mid\n0.5\nAll / Prompt\n9,000\n2 × 10−5\n4,000\n\nHigh-quality subset (400 h)\nAll three modes†\n0.3\nAll\n9,000\n2 × 10−5\n4,000\n\nTable 12: Detailed stage-wise curriculum training hyperparameters for P-MUSE. All three modes† include\nprefix-mid-suffix, prefix-mid, and mid-suffix.\nCurriculum Prompt FAD ↓ Sim. (%) ↑ F1 (%) ↑\nS1→S2→S3 Paired\nS1\nPaired\nS1→S2\nPaired\nS1→S3\nPaired\nDirect\nPaired\nS1→S2→S3 Style\nS1→S2\nStyle\nS1→S3\nStyle\nDirect\nStyle\nS1→S2→S3 Mixed\nS1→S3\nMixed\nDirect\nMixed\n\n0.835\n0.904\n0.839\n0.895\n0.763\n0.808\n0.852\n0.834\n0.871\n0.831\n0.893\n0.827\n\n93.1±.2 71.8±.8\n92.4±.2 70.9±.8\n92.6±.2 73.3±.8\n92.8±.2 72.4±.8\n92.8±.2 71.4±.8\n92.3±.2 74.4±.7\n91.7±.2 75.5±.7\n91.7±.2 74.6±.7\n91.7±.2 73.6±.8\n93.0±.2 71.8±.8\n92.6±.2 72.0±.8\n92.7±.2 71.1±.8\n\nTable 13: Editing ablation for the multi-stage CurriculumLearning. F1 denotes onset F1.\nCFG I\n\nFAD ↓ Sim. (%) ↑ Onset F1 (%) ↑\n\nfull\n[0.00, 0.60]\n[0.00, 0.80]\n[0.00, 0.92]\n[0.04, 1.00]\n[0.08, 1.00]\n\n0.835\n0.638\n0.668\n0.699\n0.769\n1.060\n\n93.1±.2\n93.0±.2\n93.0±.2\n93.1±.2\n92.3±.2\n91.2±.2\n\n71.8±.8\n72.9±.8\n72.7±.8\n72.5±.8\n71.0±.8\n70.1±.8\n\nTable 14: Additional paired-prompt editing ablations of TailDrop on P-MUSE.\n\nmode. Therefore, each instrument group contributes 600\ncases per benchmark variant, and each variant contains 2,400\ncases across bass, drum, guitar, and piano.\n\nAppendix D: Implementation Details\nArchitecture Details. P-MUSE contains 481.3M parameters in total. The generative backbone is a Diffusion Transformer (DiT) (Peebles and Xie 2023) with a hidden dimension of 1024, 25 layers, 16 attention heads (head dimension\n64). We condition the backbone on 128-dimensional mel latents and 512-dimensional MIDI tokens. We optimize the\nstandard DiT block with rotary positional embeddings (Su\net al. 2024), RMS normalization (Zhang and Sennrich 2019),\nand QK-RMSNorm (Henry et al. 2020). During training, time\nsteps are sampled from a logit-normal distribution. Following the x-prediction, v-loss configuration of JiT (Li and He\n\n2025), the DiT estimates the clean mel endpoint x̂1,θ and\nderives vθ (xt , t, h) = (x̂1,θ − xt )/(1 − t). The model is\noptimized using an L2 loss between vpred and vgt , applied\nexclusively to the target region.\nTraining Details. We optimize all stages using\nAdamW (Loshchilov and Hutter 2019) (β1 = 0.9,\nβ2 = 0.999, ϵ = 10−8 , weight decay 0.01) with gradient\nclipping set to 0.2 and a fixed random seed of 114. We\nemploy an inverse-square-root learning-rate schedule across\nall stages. For Stage 1 (S1 ICL), training runs for up to\n400,000 steps with a 32,000-step linear warmup at a peak\nlearning rate of 1 × 10−4 . For Stage 2 (S2 Prefix CFG) and\nStage 3 (S3 Unified ICL), the peak learning rate is adjusted\nto 2 × 10−5 with a 4,000-step linear warmup for up to 9,000\nsteps. Training is conducted on 8 GPUs with a batch size of\n7 per GPU.\nCurriculum-Learning Pipeline. For the SFT stages\n(Stage 2 and Stage 3), we rank training clips using the\nAudioBox evaluation model (Vyas et al. 2023) and retain a 400-hour high-quality subset, comprising 100 hours\neach of piano, guitar, bass, and drums. Stage 1 is trained\non the full training dataset across three fill-in-the-middle\n(FIM) modes (prefix-mid-suffix, prefix-mid,\nand mid-suffix) with a global MIDI drop probability\nof 0.3. Stage 2 focuses on adapting to style prompting using\nthe high-quality subset under the prefix-mid mode, with\na 0.5 MIDI drop probability which is then equally split between dropping all MIDI and prompt MIDI. Stage 3 unifies\ngeneration and editing by prepending style prompts while\nrestoring all three FIM infilling modes. The detailed stagewise curriculum configuration is summarized in Table 12.\nInference Details. At inference, we integrate the reverse\nODE using a 25-step first-order Euler solver and decode\nthe predicted mel-spectrograms with a pre-trained Vocos\nvocoder (Siuzdak 2024). For local editing, we additionally\nregenerate all audio frames within a release_duration\nwindow following the edited region to prevent residual sustain of the original audio from interfering with the suffix\naudio. During inference, release_duration is set to 1 s\nfor the paired and mixed prompt settings, whereas it is set\nto 0 s for the style prompt setting. Unless otherwise stated,\nall benchmark results use the checkpoint after multi-stage\nCurriculum-Learning and CFG (scfg = 2.0) applied at every\nintegration step.\n\n\f(a) Style prompt\nTask\n\nCFG I\n\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\n\nfull\n[0.00, 0.60]\n[0.00, 0.80]\n[0.00, 0.92]\n[0.04, 1.00]\n[0.08, 1.00]\nfull\n[0.00, 0.60]\n[0.00, 0.80]\n[0.00, 0.92]\n[0.04, 1.00]\n[0.08, 1.00]\n\n(b) Mixed prompt\n\nFAD ↓ Sim. (%)↑ Onset F1 (%)↑\n0.558\n0.468\n0.480\n0.491\n0.655\n1.780\n0.808\n0.640\n0.686\n0.704\n0.750\n1.280\n\n92.9±.5\n92.9±.4\n92.9±.5\n92.9±.5\n91.1±.6\n89.2±.6\n92.3±.2\n92.3±.2\n92.3±.2\n92.3±.2\n91.4±.2\n90.0±.2\n\n73.0±1.9\n72.9±1.9\n73.8±1.8\n73.3±1.9\n69.4±2.1\n63.0±2.2\n74.4±.7\n74.6±.7\n74.9±.7\n74.8±.7\n70.9±.8\n64.1±.9\n\nTask\n\nCFG I\n\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nGen.\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\nEdit\n\nfull\n[0.00, 0.60]\n[0.00, 0.80]\n[0.00, 0.92]\n[0.04, 1.00]\n[0.08, 1.00]\nfull\n[0.00, 0.60]\n[0.00, 0.80]\n[0.00, 0.92]\n[0.04, 1.00]\n[0.08, 1.00]\n\nFAD ↓ Sim. (%) ↑ Onset F1 (%)↑\n0.668\n0.542\n0.579\n0.572\n0.723\n1.287\n0.831\n0.615\n0.670\n0.697\n0.788\n1.006\n\n94.2±.3\n94.1±.4\n94.0±.4\n94.1±.4\n92.7±.5\n90.9±.6\n93.0±.2\n92.9±.2\n93.0±.2\n93.0±.2\n92.3±.2\n91.4±.2\n\n74.2±1.9\n74.6±1.9\n74.9±1.9\n74.7±1.9\n72.7±2.0\n69.5±2.0\n71.8±.8\n72.7±.8\n72.5±.8\n72.2±.8\n70.4±.8\n69.5±.8\n\nTable 15: Additional style- and mixed-prompt ablations of Tail-Drop on P-MUSE. (a) Style prompt; (b) Mixed prompt.\n\nAppendix E: Additional Editing Ablations\nTable 14 reports the result of the ablations of Tail-Drop\nstrategy on paired-prompt editing and Table 15 reports the\ngeneration and editing under style and mixed prompt settings. Across prompt settings and tasks, applying CFG in\nthe early interval and switching to the unconditional field\nnear the data endpoint consistently improves FAD over fulltrajectory CFG. Specifically, the interval I = [0.00, 0.60]\nachieves the best FAD across all reported groups while maintaining stable and highly competitive timbre similarity and\nonset F1 scores. In contrast, omitting early guidance (e.g.,\nI = [0.04, 1.00] and I = [0.08, 1.00]) substantially degrades performance across all three metrics. These empirical\nresults further support the theoretical insights and practical\neffectiveness of the proposed phase-aware CFG scheduling\nprinciple for Transcription-to-Audio systems like MTM and\nTTS and Tail-Drop strategy.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
